  '{"correct":["option-1","option-2"],"label":"P waves travel faster","mechanic":"evidence"}'::jsonb,
  'The earthquake generates both kinds of body wave. Compressional P waves travel faster than shear S waves and arrive first.', 'USGS: The Science of Earthquakes', 'https://www.usgs.gov/programs/earthquake-hazards/science-earthquakes'),
 ('thunder', 'why', 'Which effects of a lightning flash produce thunder?',
  '{"options":[{"id":"option-1","label":"The discharge heats the air along its path."},{"id":"option-2","label":"The heated air rapidly expands, creating a pressure wave."},{"id":"option-3","label":"Raindrops boil throughout the cloud."},{"id":"option-4","label":"The flash reflects from the ground and becomes sound."}],"mechanic":"evidence"}'::jsonb,
  '{"correct":["option-1","option-2"],"label":"Rapid heating and expansion","mechanic":"evidence"}'::jsonb,
  'Lightning heats the air so quickly that the expanding air sends out a pressure wave. We hear that wave as thunder.', 'National Weather Service: Thunder', 'https://www.weather.gov/safety/lightning-science-thunder'),
 ('lunar-eclipse', 'why', 'Why can the Moon look reddish during a total lunar eclipse?',
  '{"options":[{"id":"option-1","label":"Some sunlight reaches the Moon after passing through Earth’s atmosphere."},{"id":"option-2","label":"Earth’s atmosphere scatters shorter blue wavelengths more strongly than red ones."},{"id":"option-3","label":"The Moon glows red because its surface heats up."},{"id":"option-4","label":"The Sun turns red while Earth blocks it."}],"mechanic":"evidence"}'::jsonb,
  '{"correct":["option-1","option-2"],"label":"Sunlight filtered through Earth’s atmosphere","mechanic":"evidence"}'::jsonb,
  'Earth blocks direct sunlight during totality. The light that reaches the Moon has passed through Earth’s atmosphere, which scatters more blue light away.', 'NASA: Eclipses and the Moon', 'https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/'),
 ('lunar-eclipse', 'how', 'Order these milestones in a total lunar eclipse, starting with first contact.',
  '{"options":[{"id":"option-1","label":"The Moon begins entering the faint outer shadow."},{"id":"option-2","label":"Part of the Moon enters the dark inner shadow."},{"id":"option-3","label":"The whole Moon is inside the dark inner shadow."},{"id":"option-4","label":"The Moon fully leaves Earth’s shadow."}]}'::jsonb,
  '{"correct":["option-1","option-2","option-3","option-4"],"label":"From first contact to the end of the eclipse"}'::jsonb,
  'The Moon crosses the penumbra before entering the umbra. Totality starts once the full lunar disk is inside the umbra.', 'NASA: Eclipses and the Moon', 'https://science.nasa.gov/moon/eclipses/'),
 ('voyager-2', 'how', 'Put Voyager 2’s four giant-planet flybys in order.',
  '{"options":[{"id":"option-1","label":"Jupiter"},{"id":"option-2","label":"Saturn"},{"id":"option-3","label":"Uranus"},{"id":"option-4","label":"Neptune"}]}'::jsonb,
